TOPIC = {'slug': 'l014-026-guided-lab-sampling-and-frequency-analysis', 'title': 'Guided Lab — Sampling & Frequency Analysis', 'description': 'Apply and evaluate sampling & frequency analysis in a reproducible image-processing or computer-vision workflow, including appropriate assumptions, diagnostics, and failure analysis.', 'content_markdown': "# Guided Lab — Sampling & Frequency Analysis\n\n## Purpose\n\nThis lesson turns **Guided Lab — Sampling & Frequency Analysis** into a practical computer-vision engineering competency. The emphasis is not on memorizing an API; it is on connecting the mathematical or modeling assumption to observable image behavior, implementation choices, diagnostics, and failure modes.\n\n## Learning objective\n\nApply and evaluate sampling & frequency analysis in a reproducible image-processing or computer-vision workflow, including appropriate assumptions, diagnostics, and failure analysis.\n\n## Core concepts\n\n- sampling\n- reconstruction\n- Nyquist criterion\n- aliasing\n- quantization\n- DFT/FFT\n\n## Guided workflow\n\n1. Establish the image/model input contract: shape, channels, dtype, numeric range, coordinate convention, and preprocessing.\n2. Build the smallest useful baseline for the lesson objective.\n3. Inspect intermediate representations instead of treating the method as a black box.\n4. Change one meaningful condition or parameter and compare the result.\n5. Measure output quality when a valid reference or task metric exists.\n6. Record limitations, domain assumptions, and any distinction between evidence-preserving processing and generative/plausible synthesis.\n\n## Practice\n\n- Build an end-to-end runnable pipeline that combines the lesson's major techniques.\n- Capture intermediate visualizations, measurements, and failure cases instead of reporting only the final image.\n- Compare at least two method or parameter choices and justify the selected configuration.\n\n## Debugging checklist\n\n- Verify channel order and image range before blaming the algorithm.\n- Verify row/column versus x/y coordinate conventions.\n- Check border, padding, interpolation, mask, or resize behavior explicitly.\n- For frequency-domain work, check shift conventions and complex-valued outputs.\n- For pretrained models, use the checkpoint's preprocessing contract.\n- For generative outputs, do not equate plausibility with factual recovery.\n\n## Evidence boundary\n\nCode in this curriculum export is a **lesson seed and curriculum handoff**. The package validates Python syntax and structural contracts. It does not claim every external model, API, GPU workflow, dataset, or optional dependency was executed end-to-end.\n", 'estimated_minutes': 75, 'prerequisite_ids': []}
